{"name":"Swamp Sessions","hex":"#93977f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"swamp","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An area of wet (water-saturated), spongy (soft) land, often with trees, generally a rich ecosystem for certain plants and animals but ill-suited for many agricultural purposes.","A place or situation that is foul or where progress is difficult."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To drench or fill with water.","To overwhelm; to make too busy, or overrun the capacity of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/swamp"},{"term":"sessions","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of session"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sessions"}],"entries":[{"title":"Swamp","blocks":[{"text":"A swamp is a forested wetland. Swamps are considered to be transition zones because both land and water play a role in creating this environment. Swamps vary in size and are located all around the world. The water of a swamp may be fresh water, brackish water, or seawater. Freshwater swamps form along large rivers or lakes where they are critically dependent upon rainwater and seasonal flooding to maintain natural water level fluctuations. Saltwater swamps are found along tropical and subtropical coastlines. Some swamps have hammocks, or dry-land protrusions, covered by aquatic vegetation, or vegetation that tolerates periodic inundation or soil saturation. The two main types of swamp are \"true\" or swamp forests and \"transitional\" or shrub swamps. In the boreal regions of Canada, the word swamp is colloquially used for what is more formally termed a bog, fen, or muskeg. Some of the world's largest swamps are found along major rivers such as the Amazon, the Mississippi, and the Congo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Florida_freshwater_swamp_usgov_image.jpg/330px-Florida_freshwater_swamp_usgov_image.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swamp"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Coastal_marsh_profile.png/500px-Coastal_marsh_profile.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Coastal_marsh_profile.png/500px-Coastal_marsh_profile.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Infographic demonstrating transition between swamp and marsh, among the nomenclature of other portions of a costal wetland environment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoastal_marsh_profile.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg/1280px-Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Linnaistensuo Mire, a nature reserve swamp in Lahti, Finland.[9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALinnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Marsh_Arabs_in_a_mashoof.jpg/500px-Marsh_Arabs_in_a_mashoof.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Marsh_Arabs_in_a_mashoof.jpg/1280px-Marsh_Arabs_in_a_mashoof.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Marsh_A